A||N||21||Monkeyman||Looking for jobs (relocation)||||9||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||11-15-2001||05:24 AM||||My wife and I are looking for new jobs and would like to relocate.  Here's my sob story (grab some Kleenex).<P>My wife was the assistant general manager for a small motel for 3 years.  The motel is under new management and they claim that they couldn't afford to keep her so she got laid off a couple of weeks ago.<P>I manage a C store/gas station (1 of a large chain).  I've been at my current job for 20 months.  I don't like my job (very long hours/crappy pay for the hours, etc) but it's about the only thing in my area.  We had a physical inventory of the store yesterday (regular happening every 12 weeks or so).  Let's just say that things didn't go well.  While I'm not the one who caused the poor results, I'm still the manager and must take full responsibility for whatever happens in my store, whether I like it or not.  (This is the upper managements opinion and I happen to agree.)  I'm now in danger of losing my job (or at least getting very demoted, which would amount to termination).<P>My wife and I have been discussing relocation as there aren't any decent jobs in this area.  We struggle with money every month as it is and we'd like to make our lives a little better (i.e. more money=less stress).  I've looked on monster.com but haven't really come up with anything promising.  Most companies that pay well require a degree which neither of us has.  We own a house so we would need to sell that.  The cost of relocation may be more than we can handle (the actual move, renting a place (read: deposit $ we don't have) until we can buy, etc, etc).  <P>Does anyone have any suggestions?  Possibly some great jobs in your area?  My wife's family is from the Cincinnati area so that would be a plus, but we aren't ruling out any place at this point.  Neither of us are factory people (I've tried and quit a few--just not for me).<P>Sorry for the bandwidth, but I don't know where to turn at this point.  I was hoping my job would last forever (or at least long enough for us to figure out what to do), but...<P>Dave||||reg||9
Z||000001||Monkeyman||11-15-2001||05:28 AM||||Ignore all of the grammatical errors.  I'm tired and stressed out and don't feel like editing the entire post.  Thx||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||11-15-2001||06:44 AM||||Is it possible you could move in with relatives in the Cincinatti area?At least long enough to get established into your own place.I don't know the area,but it may be better than what you face now.I can't really offer any help other than that sugestion.Best of luck.||||reg||14
Z||000003||Jake_Dragon||11-15-2001||06:59 AM||||A can do attitude and experience will take you as far as a degree.<BR>Sometimes you need to just take a chance and talk to prospective employees, if nothing else you get practice interviewing.<BR>Things are tight right now but I think that if you hold on till spring the economy will be in a better state. Some times you have to work crappy jobs till the right one comes along. <BR>It took me 15 years but I am pretty happy with my job, I am sure something will come along just try to keep a positive outlook and you will be fine.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fformula88||11-15-2001||10:16 AM||||I was in the job market for about a year after I graduated until this summer when I finally got a job (graduated May 2000).  I really wanted to relocate too and found it difficult to do at a distance.  I'd say the majority of companies and recruiters I talked to really weren't interested in people from areas outside of where they were located unless I already had a firm comitment to move (like already had an app't or place to stay).  I think if you really want to relocate the best thing to do would be to send yourself, or your wife to wherever you want to go to start job hunting a little... maybe for a week or two.  It gets expensive, but once yyou have a foot in some doors you can continue to talk and possibly travel for interviews as needed.  The best thing would be to totally move to where you want to be, but of course thats rather expensive without a job.<P>I do not like Monster.com.  Its almost all headhunter companies advertising.  try looking up newspapers where you want to move.  Nearly all have the classifieds online now.  Also, your best bets are probably cities with the best job markets... especially with the slowing economy.  <P>Good luck!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Voytek||11-15-2001||10:33 AM||||Hey<BR>I'm sorry to hear that.  I can imagine how that feels (the pressure, the unknown, etc.)<P>I won't be much help because I assume moving up to Canada isn't exactly a possibility.  Canadian economy is actually in a pretty good state (still) and we have quite a few job postings all over town.<P>Formula is right - once things pick up, there will be a lot more jobs.  I don't know much about your situation, but maybe you can tough it out for a bit?<P>Maybe this little bit will help: when things aren't looking up, I always think:'am I at the bottom of the barrel?  Are there people who have it even worse?'  The answers are 'no' and 'yes', respectively.  It usually cheers me up.<P>Good luck in any case.  Life has ups and downs - your ups are still ahead.||||reg||1
Z||000006||DaRkLoRD||11-15-2001||10:44 AM||||I wish I could help, I work full time as a computer tech. at a nuclear training center.. for CDN $10/hr.. I've been looking for a new job as well, but there's not much available in my area either. and to top it all off, I'm back living with my parents again... nor for long though. it's driving me nuts!<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||87GTZ34||11-15-2001||11:02 AM||||MM, Right now it is a seller's (employer's) market for jobs.  I'm sure you're already aware of this but I wanted to point out that headhunters and placement agencies know that the employer's don't want to relocate new hires right now so they don't spend their energy on them.  I don't know if you have kids but most likely you will have to two-step a move...  Meaning, since you are working, identify a desired area (community, jobs, size, housing  etc.) your wife will have to find something there and them move just her, then once she is stable close-up and you move.||||reg||1
Z||000008||84Bill||11-15-2001||11:09 AM||||Well MM look in the Atlanta and Charlott NC areas they have been TECH hotbeds for some time and where there is Tech people there are hotels for the traveling masses.<BR> ||||reg||14
Z||000009||Philphine||11-15-2001||12:08 PM||||i can't help you at all but i hope you pull through. also hoping it dosen't stress your relationship with your wife. try to stay a team.  <P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000010||MinnGreenGT||11-15-2001||12:11 PM||||MM- recieved and replied to your email as well... I'm here to help however I can.  Exactly what kind of job are you looking for?  Retail management OK?  OfficeMax (my former employer) was always looking for good managers- and you mentioned Cincinnati, their HQ is located in Shaker Heights. OMAX is still looking in this area as well.  Let us know what kind of jobs you're looking for and we'll keep our eyes peeled.<P>This is the link for one of Minnesota's (and nationwide) job search systems: <A HREF="http://careers.twincities.com/xp/_780023_/index.xml" TARGET=_blank>http://careers.twincities.com/xp/_780023_/index.xml</A>   (They recently acquired Headhunter.net)||||reg||1
Z||000011||Pheagey||11-15-2001||01:06 PM||||I hear that the Wal*Mart ditrobution centers are always hiring. Decent pay 10/11+ an hour (but 12Hr. shifts). Sorry I could be more help. Hope everythign works well for you thought. Never give up never surrender.<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed.<BR>"The car formerly know as Dj's Fiero."||||reg||1
Z||000012||Raydar||11-15-2001||01:26 PM||||There a lots of people and lots of places to work in and around Atlanta. You don't want to live or work in the city or in the close-in southern 'burbs, due to the crime. <BR>Economy is kind of slow due to the technology downturn, but not as bad as other places.<BR>Cost of housing is kinda high, but not like NY or California.<BR>Summers are hot (90s). Winters are mild (lows in teens or 20's only a few days).<BR>Drivers are obnoxious. Gas prices are among the cheapest in the US. (89.9/gal, today.)<P>Did you decide not to drive, any more?<BR>Regardless, I wish you the best.<P>Raydar||||reg||1
Z||000013||Cheever3000||11-15-2001||01:55 PM||||Houston and surrounding areas are growing like mad.  It's a thriving place with lots of jobs. And we're just starting the really beautiful season of weather now that summer is over, going to be perfect until about next June or July.  Here's the first place I go to look for Houston jobs... <A HREF="http://www.chron.com/class/jobs/search.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.chron.com/class/jobs/search.html</A> <P>Salaries are good here, but housing and cost of living are definitely lower than Cincinnati (I know!).  Good luck whatever you do.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Oreif||11-15-2001||02:31 PM||||Try these links. They are local newspapers that have On-line listings and resume services. Found lots of jobs under "Retail" and "Manager" <P> <A HREF="http://classified1.dailyherald.com/index.asp?404;http://classified1.dailyherald.com/class/classified.asp" TARGET=_blank>http://classified1.dailyherald.com/index.asp?404;http://classified1.da  ilyherald.com/class/classified.asp</A> <P><BR> <A HREF="http://careers.chicagotribune.com/xp/_790004_/index.xml" TARGET=_blank>http://careers.chicagotribune.com/xp/_790004_/index.xml</A> <P><BR> <A HREF="http://dir.yahoo.com/Regional/U_S__States/Illinois/Cities/Chicago/Employment/Jobs/" TARGET=_blank>http://dir.yahoo.com/Regional/U_S__States/Illinois/Cities/Chicago/Employment/Jobs/</A> <P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">            <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT><P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 11-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Monkeyman||11-15-2001||04:59 PM||||Wow!  Thanx for the replies, guys.  It's nice to know that there are some of you who care.  I'll check out some of your suggestions.  Since my wife has family in the Cincinnati area, that would be her 1<sup>st</sup> choice, but I'm guessing that if it comes down to it, she'll consider other possibilities.  I've spoken to Oreif about this already.  He's given me some help.  All of my family is local so it doesn't matter where I go.  The south is nice in the winter, but gets too warm in the summer.  The north is too cold in the winter, but great in the summer.  I guess every place has it's advantages.  (Just thinking out loud.)  <P>Thanx again for the help.  Keep the tips coming.  If you hear of a job (generally management related for me and hotel/motel related for my wife), LMK.<P>Dave||||reg||1
