Z||000018||Formula||11-16-2001||09:15 AM||||search <A HREF="http://www.kdka.com" TARGET=_blank>www.kdka.com</A>  for pittsburgh area jobs.  Lots of tech plaes that if you got your foot in the door you'l move up quick.  I know there are a lot of people in my school that had jobs in the coal, steel plants until they closed and now get unemployment for 2 years while they get an associate degree for a tech field.  Maybe you could look into that.  My school is <A HREF="http://www.fe.psu.edu" TARGET=_blank>www.fe.psu.edu</A>  if your interested.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Raydar||11-16-2001||07:22 PM||||Dave,<P>Just for grins, go to one of the local Motorola two-way radio shops. With all the stereo equipment you've swapped around, you could probably get a job as an installer, putting radios and emergency lights in cop cars and such. It's steady work. Most departments are always rotating at least some of their vehicles. And it's usually 8-5, M-F.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000020||Monkeyman||11-16-2001||07:44 PM||||Raydar--Good idea, but I really don't know squat about stereos/radios/etc.  I'm a quick learner, but nobody is going to give someone a chance to learn something on the job.  Either you have to have experience or education.  Unfortunately, I have neither (in almost any field except trucking, which I don't want to get back into at this time, and management).<P>Jim--I'll email you this weekend.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/patriot.gif">    <BR><B><FONT COLOR="red">Proud</FONT> <FONT COLOR="white">to be an</FONT> <FONT COLOR="blue">American</FONT>!!</B>||||reg||1
Z||000021||Sage||11-16-2001||11:54 PM||||Dave;<P>Very sorry to learn of your situation.  Been through it myself on occasion.  Job market around here is pretty grim, unless you want to start at the bottom pay wise or have an "inside person" or a list of credentials the length of your arm. Since you have retail experience and are looking at Cinci. for relocation, check out the BP stations in and around Cinci..  I pretty much got the manager of one of our local stations her job with them 8 years ago, she is very happy with them and makes good money.  The co. just changed hands again and she says the new owners are good to work for and the pay at management level is not bad.  Don't know exactly what you want to do, but when you gotta do something, sometimes you do what is available.  <P>I also have a cousin that is a Project Superintendent at a place called "Bi-Con Services".  I believe they build bridges or something along those lines.  He is out on the road quite a bit, but they have a manufacturing facility with regular hour jobs.  Saw him several months ago and he said they were looking for welders at that time.  Addy is: Bi-Con Services, Inc.<BR>                10901 Clay Pike Road<BR>                Derwent, Ohio    43733<BR>PH-(740) 685-2542<BR>FX-(740) 685-3863<P>Don't know what the current status for them is, but they pay pretty good for blue collar work.<P>Do you have a recent, good looking well prepared resume?  Kind of like selling a manuscript, you have to get in many people's hands sometimes before the right one recognizes it as a potential money maker.  <P>I did retail for a number of years, that's how I know I don't like it!  It takes a special type person to be good at it, and enjoy it. Even worked for BP before they were BP, Standard Oil of Ohio, in fact that was one of my first jobs after high school.  Worked there while going to Community college, but that was 35 or so years ago and completely off the subject!<P>Go to the magazine stand and get some out of town newspapers from places you might be interested in, or use information or phone book, and call the papers, they will usually mail to potential customers.  Find some opportunities that interest you, then start sending out that resume.  A follow up to that isn't a bad idea either.  Go to your local state employment agency, they usually have a job search package that outlines thier method for finding gainful employment.<P>Good luck any way you go, and let us know if you find something great.<P>Or you could invent something that everybody wants or needs!?!?<P><P>------------------<BR>Have a good one!||||reg||1
